W =  rac{\sum_{i=1}^{n} (x_i -  ar{x})^2}{\sum_{i=1}^{n} (x_i -  ar{x})^2 + \sum_{i=1}^{n} (x_i -  ar{x})^2}

where x_i is the ith observation in the sample,  ar{x} is the mean of the sample, and n is the number of observations in the sample. The Shapiro-Wilk statistic is then compared to a critical value, which is determined by the sample size and the significance level. If the Shapiro-Wilk statistic is greater than the critical value, the distribution is considered to be sufficiently close to normal for practical purposes. In this case, the Shapiro-Wilk statistic is 0.95, which is greater than the critical value of 0.95, so the distribution of income inequality is considered to be sufficiently close to normal for practical purposes.""}
